---
// CodeFence.astro - the shiki code fence for the hand-written pages: the
// block renders through the same shiki pipeline the docs markdown fences use
// (the one theme, Source/Library/ShikiTheme.ts, wired into astro.config.ts),
// so a command block gets the identical .astro-code markup the docs' ```
// fences get - the token spans, the data-language tag, the same palette. The
// code string is rendered byte-exact; only the rendering changes.
//
// PRESENTATION lives in two places, neither of them here: the palette is the
// theme (inline styles a stylesheet cannot outrank), and the frame, padding,
// rhythm, hover and copy chip are the plate in Source/Stylesheet/Global.css +
// Source/Script/CodePlate.ts. This component therefore emits the same bare
// `<pre class="astro-code">` Astro's markdown pipeline emits, and the plate
// treats both identically - so `font-mono`/`text-base`/`[&>pre]:p-3.5` are
// gone: they were a second, drifting definition of the same block.
//
// ONE COPY CONTROL, AND THE PLATE OWNS IT. Source/Script/CodePlate.ts builds
// the `.zine-plate-bar` on every fence: the language tag (metadata) plus the
// block's copy chip (the action). This component must therefore never render a
// second control for the same payload. It used to, and the result was two
// chips in one cluster - one of them labelled `BASH` because its label
// defaulted to `Lang.toUpperCase()`, a language name sitting where an action
// belongs, beside a plate chip that said `COPY`. There is now exactly one
// control per fence; its label names the ACTION (`Copy`, or `Copy command`
// where the clipboard payload differs from the transcript); and the language
// appears only in the metadata tag, which is not a button and does not look
// like one.
//
// `Copy` is therefore a DECLARATION, not a control. The plate gives every
// block a copy chip anyway, so `Copy` asks for what the plate already does and
// cannot add a second one. It stays accepted because the call sites that pass
// it are recording a real intent - this block is meant to be copied - and that
// intent is worth reading at the page.
//
// `CopyLabel` is accepted and IGNORED, for the reason the label changed: every
// call site passed a language (`"BASH"`, `Lane.Shell.toUpperCase()`), and a
// language name on an action is the defect, not the configuration.
//
// ONE PAYLOAD, ONE TRUTH. The payload is a prop and there is exactly one of
// it: `CopyPayload` when set, `Code` otherwise. It travels to the plate as
// `data-plate-payload` on the wrapper, and the plate copies that string
// instead of `pre.textContent` - so the one chip copies the one payload and
// two controls can never disagree about what the block means. By default the
// payload IS `Code`: the fence never takes a second copy of the command, so
// the chip cannot copy something the reader is not looking at.
//
// `CopyPayload` exists because a fence is allowed to be a TRANSCRIPT while its
// copy must be a COMMAND. An install block that still carries `$` prompts and
// `//` annotations renders exactly as written, and a reader who pastes it gets
// a shell that cannot run it. Pass the bare line as `CopyPayload` and the
// block stays a faithful picture of a terminal without the clipboard
// inheriting the picture - and the chip reads `Copy command`, because the
// clipboard no longer holds the transcript on screen. Prefer modelling the
// block as data (Source/pages/setup.astro's InstallStep) so the two never have
// to differ at all; this prop is for the fences that legitimately must show
// more than they copy.
//
// ONE FRAME PER REGION. The plate draws the frame, so `Class` is LAYOUT ONLY -
// spacing and width. Frame utilities are dropped here rather than passed
// through: a `border` on this wrapper painted a second border around the plate
// with the plate's own margin showing between the two, which is exactly the
// "extra padding inside the frame" a reader sees. The wrapper keeps a stable
// `.zine-fence` name so a panel that already frames or pads can zero the
// plate's margin for the fences inside it (Global.css, CODE PLATES).
//
// THE RIBBON HAS THREE SLOTS AND ALL THREE ARE ALWAYS FILLED. The ribbon is a
// row of METADATA with one action at its right edge - leading field, language,
// copy - and the leading field names the block's SOURCE, in two truthful
// readings:
//
//   `Path` set      the file the block was taken from:
//                   `aphrodite.toml  TOML  Copy`
//   `Path` unset    what the block IS:
//                   `Command`, `Transcript` or `Snippet` - `Snippet  TOML  Copy`
//
// `Path` is a prop rather than something the plate could infer, because a
// fence has no idea where its bytes came from: the page that pasted them does.
// Unset is the normal case (a command has no path), so the field falls back to
// the kind this component CAN derive from its own props: a block its page
// declares `Copy` is a command to run, a block carrying `CopyPayload` is a
// transcript whose clipboard holds the bare command, and anything else is a
// snippet. Two readings of one slot, never both at once, and no call site has
// to change to get a full ribbon. Source/Script/CodePlate.ts builds the ribbon
// and reads this as `data-plate-path`; this component still renders no control
// of its own.
//
// ONE COMMAND PER WELL, AND THE RESULT IT SHOULD PRODUCE UNDER IT. The block
// this component carries is an INTERACTIVE CLI SNIPPET: a recessed well holding
// ONE command, the copy affordance in its ribbon confirming the copy IN PLACE
// (the plate's chip is the block's only control, and the confirmation is the
// control's own label - see `Component/CopyButton.astro`), and a CALLOUT BANNER
// under the well carrying what the reader should see when it works.
//
// `Result` renders that banner: a hairline, a label, and the expected output in
// the technical voice, byte-exact. It is the expected RESULT and nothing else -
// the clipboard still holds `CopyPayload` (or `Code`), so a banner never
// changes what the control copies, and the copy payload stays byte-exact.
//
// ONE FRAME PER REGION, still. The banner draws a TOP HAIRLINE and no other
// edge: the region's frame belongs to the page (a `.shell-card`, a
// `border-2 border-oxblood-dark bg-soot-black` body), and a banner with four
// edges under a well would be a second frame around the same region - the
// defect the flush rule in Global.css exists to prevent. Inside such a body the
// banner spans the body's full width and the well's own 0.5rem inset is the gap
// above the hairline, which is the footer-callout shape the pages already use
// (Source/pages/plugin.astro).
//
// ONE COMMAND, NOT A SEQUENCE. A well carries one command because the ribbon
// says `Command` and the chip says `Copy command`: a reader who pastes it gets
// one thing to run. A multi-step install is a SEQUENCE - model it as data (the
// setup page's `InstallStep`) and let its own surface carry the steps, or the
// single `Copy command` claim is false.
import { codeToHtml } from "shiki";

import { ZineTheme } from "@Library/ShikiTheme";

interface Props {
	Code: string;
	Lang?: string;
	Class?: string;
	/**
	 * Declare that this block is meant to be copied. The plate already gives
	 * every fence a copy chip, so this adds no second control - it records the
	 * intent at the call site, and it is what makes the ribbon's leading field
	 * read `Command` instead of `Snippet`: a block a page declares copyable is
	 * a command to run, not a fragment to read.
	 */
	Copy?: boolean;
	/**
	 * Accepted and ignored. A control's label names the action, never the
	 * language; every call site passed a language name here.
	 *
	 * @deprecated The plate's chip owns the label.
	 */
	CopyLabel?: string;
	/**
	 * The clipboard payload when it must differ from the rendered text: the
	 * bare command for a block that displays a transcript. Unset = `Code`.
	 */
	CopyPayload?: string;
	/**
	 * The file the block was taken from, printed as the ribbon's leading
	 * field. Metadata, never an action: it sits beside the language tag and
	 * the copy chip, and nothing about it is clickable.
	 *
	 * A real path always wins over the derived kind, and an empty string
	 * counts as unset - a blank leading field would leave the ribbon with two
	 * slots and an invisible gap where the first one belongs.
	 */
	Path?: string;
	/**
	 * The expected result of running this block - what a reader should see when
	 * it worked. Rendered byte-exact in the callout banner under the well.
	 *
	 * Unset renders NO banner: a transcript, a config file or a fragment has no
	 * "expected result", and an empty banner is a frame with nothing in it.
	 */
	Result?: string;
	/**
	 * The banner's label - the eyebrow that says the line beside it is the
	 * result of the command rather than more of it. Defaults to `EXPECTED`.
	 */
	ResultLabel?: string;
}

const {
	Code,
	Lang = "bash",
	Class = "",
	Copy,
	CopyPayload,
	Path,
	Result,
	ResultLabel = "EXPECTED",
} = Astro.props;

// THE RIBBON'S LEADING FIELD, ranked by how strong the fact is: a path the
// page declared, else the kind this component can prove from its own props.
// `CopyPayload` outranks `Copy` because a transcript that copies a bare
// command is a transcript first - the clipboard is the command, the block is
// not. An empty `Path` is unset, not a leading field of zero width.
const Kind = CopyPayload !== undefined ? "Transcript" : Copy ? "Command" : "Snippet";
const Source = Path !== undefined && Path !== "" ? Path : Kind;

// The plate owns the frame - border, radius, background, shadow, hover lift.
// `Class` carries the layout half only, so the frame utilities that used to be
// passed here are dropped: they were a second frame around a plate that
// already has one.
const Frame = /^(border|rounded|ring|shadow|outline|bg-)/;
const Layout = Class.split(/\s+/)
	.filter((Token) => Token !== "" && !Frame.test(Token))
	.join(" ");

// Astro's markdown pipeline renames shiki's `shiki` class to `astro-code` and
// adds the data-language attribute; the component applies the same
// normalization so the markup matches the docs' fences. The overflow hint is
// NOT added here - `body pre.astro-code` owns overflow-x, one definition.
const Html = (await codeToHtml(Code, { lang: Lang, theme: ZineTheme }))
	.replace('class="shiki ', 'class="astro-code ')
	.replace("<pre ", `<pre data-language="${Lang}" `);
---

<div
	class:list={["zine-fence", Layout]}
	data-plate-payload={CopyPayload}
	data-plate-path={Source}
>
	<!-- The fence itself: the same bare `<pre class="astro-code">` Astro's
	     markdown pipeline emits. `Source/Script/CodePlate.ts` wraps it in the
	     well and hangs the ribbon on it; a page that declares `Result` gets the
	     banner below as the block's last row. -->
	<Fragment set:html={Html} />
	{Result && (
		<div class="zine-callout flex min-w-0 flex-col gap-2 border-t border-[color:var(--tier-well-edge)] px-3 py-3 sm:flex-row sm:items-baseline sm:gap-4">
			<span class="telemetry-tag shrink-0">{ResultLabel}</span>
			<code class="type-code min-w-0 break-words whitespace-pre-wrap text-bone-newsprint">{Result}</code>
		</div>
	)}
</div>
